{"name":"Smoked Oak Brown","hex":"#573f16","fetched":"2026-10-16","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"smoked","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Adjective","definitions":["Of food, treated with smoke, often for flavor or as a method of preservation.","Of glass, tinted."]},{"part":"Verb","definitions":["simple past and past participle of smoke"]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/smoked"},{"term":"oak","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Symbol","definitions":["ISO 639-3 language code for Noakhali."]},{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A deciduous tree with distinctive deeply lobed leaves, acorns, and notably strong wood, typically of England and northeastern North America, included in genus Quercus.","The wood of the oak."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/oak"},{"term":"brown","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A colour like that of chocolate or coffee.","One of the colour balls used in snooker, with a value of 4 points."]},{"part":"Adjective","definitions":["Having a brown color/colour.","Gloomy."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/brown"}],"entries":[{"title":"Ammonia fuming","blocks":[{"text":"Ammonia fuming is a wood-finishing process which darkens wood and brings out the grain pattern. Fuming involves exposing the wood to fumes from a strong aqueous solution of ammonium hydroxide which reacts with the tannins in the wood. The process works best on white oak because of the high tannin-content of this wood. Fumed oak is also called fumigated oak or smoked oak. Other species may also be fumed, but usually will not darken as much as white oak. In America, the introduction of the process is usually associated with the activities of the furniture-maker Gustav Stickley at the beginning of the 20th century, but fuming was known in Europe before this."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/b5/Clonfert_Cathedral_Choir_Stalls_South_2009_09_17.jpg/330px-Clonfert_Cathedral_Choir_Stalls_South_2009_09_17.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ammonia_fuming","wikidata":"Q2178602"},{"title":"Oak","blocks":[{"text":"An oak is a hardwood tree or shrub in the genus Quercus of the beech family. They have spirally arranged leaves, often with lobed edges, and a nut called an acorn, borne within a cup. The genus is widely distributed in the Northern Hemisphere, with some 475 species, both deciduous and evergreen. Fossil oaks date back to the Middle Eocene. Molecular phylogeny shows that the genus is divided into Old World and New World clades, but many oak species hybridise freely, making the genus's history difficult to resolve."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/a/af/Quercus_robur.jpg/330px-Quercus_robur.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Oak","wikidata":"Q12004"},{"title":"Brown","blocks":[{"text":"Brown is a color. It can be thought as a darker, typically desaturated shade of orange, and can often be produced by combining red and yellow with another color, namely blue."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/ca/Espresso-roasted_coffee_beans.jpg/330px-Espresso-roasted_coffee_beans.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Brown","wikidata":"Q47071"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/b5/Clonfert_Cathedral_Choir_Stalls_South_2009_09_17.jpg/500px-Clonfert_Cathedral_Choir_Stalls_South_2009_09_17.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/b5/Clonfert_Cathedral_Choir_Stalls_South_2009_09_17.jpg/1280px-Clonfert_Cathedral_Choir_Stalls_South_2009_09_17.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/b5/Clonfert_Cat